# Zendesk

Call the Zendesk Support API as the connected user via the bundled helper. **You
are already authenticated** — the egress proxy injects the user's Zendesk token
on the wire, so never pass credentials yourself.

This workspace is connected to **{{APP:base_url}}**. Pass that account to every
command as `--site`.

{{ACTION_AVAILABILITY_SECTION}}

## Usage

    python .opencode/skills/zendesk/zendesk_api.py --site <account> <command> [args]

`<account>` is the subdomain (`acme`) or the account URL
(`https://acme.zendesk.com`). Read commands auto-paginate and prune empty
fields. `python zendesk_api.py --site <account> <command> -h` shows a command's
flags.

### Find tickets

`search` takes [Zendesk search syntax](https://support.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408886879258)
and is the way to answer most questions.

```
python zendesk_api.py --site acme search "type:ticket status:open tags:billing" [--limit N]
python zendesk_api.py --site acme search "type:ticket created>2026-10-01" --sort-by created_at --sort-order desc
```

### Read one ticket

```
python zendesk_api.py --site acme ticket 12345
python zendesk_api.py --site acme comments 12345
python zendesk_api.py --site acme metrics 12345
```

### What changed since last time

For a scheduled task, poll the incremental export rather than re-searching the
whole backlog. Start from a time, then resume from the `cursor` the previous
run returned — that is what makes a repeating task skip nothing and re-read
nothing.

```
python zendesk_api.py --site acme changed --since 2026-10-01T00:00:00Z [--limit N]
python zendesk_api.py --site acme changed --cursor <cursor from last run>
```

`--since` takes a unix timestamp or an ISO-8601 instant and must be at least a
minute in the past. Pass exactly one of `--since` and `--cursor`. The result
carries `cursor` and `end_of_stream`: keep calling with the new `cursor` until
`end_of_stream` is true, and store that last `cursor` for the next run. Here
`--limit` is a floor — the export stops at a page boundary, so the `cursor` is
always safe to resume from.

### Queues, people, and feedback

```
python zendesk_api.py --site acme views
python zendesk_api.py --site acme view-tickets 360001 [--limit N]
python zendesk_api.py --site acme satisfaction [--score bad] [--limit N]
python zendesk_api.py --site acme user me
python zendesk_api.py --site acme organization 42
python zendesk_api.py --site acme fields
```

### Write

```
python zendesk_api.py --site acme create --subject "Billing error" --body "..." [--tags billing,urgent]
python zendesk_api.py --site acme update 12345 --set status=solved --set priority=high
python zendesk_api.py --site acme comment 12345 --body "Looked into this." [--public]
python zendesk_api.py --site acme tag 12345 --tags needs-review [--replace]
```

## Output

JSON on stdout. List commands return `{"ok": true, "<collection>": [...],
"count": N, "truncated": bool}` (`truncated` means more results existed past
`--limit`). `tag` returns the ticket's resulting tag list, the other writes
return the ticket. HTTP failures return `{"ok": false, "status": <code>, "error":
"<message>"}` and exit non-zero — surface the error rather than retrying
blindly. A `429` also carries `retry_after_seconds`; wait that long before the
next call.

## Notes

- Tickets come back as a readable subset of their fields. `--full` keeps every
  field, `--raw` keeps empty ones. A `search` hit that is a user or an
  organization is never cut down.
- A comment is a ticket update in Zendesk: `comment` writes an internal note by
  default, and `--public` makes it a reply the requester sees.
- `--set key=value` is repeatable and takes Zendesk ticket field names
  (`status`, `priority`, `assignee_id`, `group_id`). A value that parses as JSON
  keeps its type, so `--set assignee_id=123` sends a number.
- `tag` adds tags; `--replace` overwrites the whole tag set. Zendesk treats a
  tag change as a ticket update, so it follows the "Update a ticket" setting,
  and it refuses tag changes on a closed ticket.
- `call` lets you hit any v2 endpoint: `call <METHOD> /api/v2/... [json_body]`.
  Pass user input as the JSON body, not by string-formatting into the path.
